%S A060841 1,4,18,144,900,16200,132300,2116800,28576800,714420000,8644482000,
%T A060841 311201352000,4382752374000,143169910884000,4026653743612500,
%U A060841 128852919795600000,2327405863808025000,125679916645633350000,2520580550504091075000,126029027525204553750000
%N A060841 Numerator of 1/det(M) where M is the n X n matrix with M[i,j] = 1/lcm(i,j).
%C A060841 The value of 1/det(M) is not always an integer! For example, 1/det(35) = 5029296746186844716050163189085401314000634765625/2. - _Harry J. Smith_, Jul 13 2009
%C A060841 Conjecture: 1/det(M) is an integer only for n: 1 - 34, 36 and 38. All denominators are powers of two (A000079). But not all powers of two are present. See A260502. - _Robert G. Wilson v_, Aug 02 2015
%C A060841 Values of n at which a(n) = a(n+1): 63, 127, 255, ..., . - _Robert G. Wilson v_, Aug 03 2015
%C A060841 Adamczewski disproves the above conjecture with counterexample for n = 1807, in linked article. - _Michael De Vlieger_, Aug 20 2026
%C A060841 The first assertion in the conjecture of _Robert G. Wilson v_ above (that 1/det(M) is an integer only for n = 1..34, 36 and 38) is true; the counterexample n = 1807 concerns only the second assertion, on the denominators. Indeed 1/det(M) = (n!)^2/(phi(1)*phi(2)*...*phi(n)) (see the formula), and for n = 35, n = 37 and all n >= 39 the exponent of 2 in phi(1)*...*phi(n) is larger than the exponent of 2 in (n!)^2, so 1/det(M) is not an integer: the odd primes p <= 97 (each contributing floor(n/p) times the exponent of 2 in p-1) together with the powers of 2 from 4 to 128 already give enough factors of 2. The proof is formalized in Lean 4 and machine-checked; see the links. - _Daniel Veljjanoski_, Nov 10 2026
%H A060841 Robert G. Wilson v, <a href="/A060841/b060841.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..400</a>
%H A060841 Tom Adamczewski, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.11941">OEIS Open: How many conjectures can language models turn into theorems?</a>, arXiv:2608.11941 [cs.AI], 2026. See p. 19 (proof, Appendix A).
%H A060841 Daniel Veljjanoski, <a href="https://github.com/veljjanoski/oeis60841-palomar">Lean 4 proof that 1/det(M) is an integer exactly for n <= 34, 36 and 38</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%H A060841 Daniel Veljjanoski, <a href="https://palomar-registry.org/entry.html?id=PALOMAR-2026-11-10-000008">Palomar registry entry PALOMAR-2026-11-10-000008</a>, 2026.
%F A060841 a(n) = numerator of (n!)^2 / (phi(1)*phi(2)*...*phi(n)) = numerator of (n!)^2 / A001088(n). - Corrected by _Daniel Veljjanoski_, Nov 10 2026
%e A060841 a(2) = 4 because the matrix M is [1,1/2; 1/2,1/2] and det(M) = 1/4.
%t A060841 d[n_] := Denominator[ Det[ Table[ GCD[1/i, 1/j], {i, n}, {j, n}]]; Array[d, 18]] (* _Robert G. Wilson v_, Aug 02 2015 *)
%o A060841 (PARI) vector(20, n, numerator(1/matdet(matrix(n, n, i, j, 1/lcm(i,j))))) \\ _Michel Marcus_, Aug 03 2015
